1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,607
A green @WebMvcTest can still hide a
broken @Transactional boundary or a
security matcher that rejects the booth
scanner.

2
00:00:07,727 --> 00:00:19,103
Integration tests load more of the real
Boot application — often with
@SpringBootTest and MockMvc or a random-
port TestRestTemplate — and exercise the
gate release flow as collaborators wire
together.

3
00:00:19,223 --> 00:00:30,719
Look at this java for a moment. Read what
is real and what is faked. Spring
Security, MVC, the ledger repository, and
transactional proxies are real.

4
00:00:30,839 --> 00:00:42,575
Billing stays a @MockBean so the test does
not need a live billing pod — you are
proving gate’s flow, not billing’s SQL.
Assert side effects in the ledger, not
only HTTP 202.

5
00:00:42,695 --> 00:00:53,783
The billing-failure case proves the
transactional boundary: no ledger row when
quoting dies mid-flow. Anonymous access
must fail if the booth scanner is supposed
to authenticate.

6
00:00:53,903 --> 00:01:01,607
Random-port style catches serialization
and client configuration that MockMvc can
miss: Look at this java for a moment.

7
00:01:01,727 --> 00:01:09,024
Keep integration profiles pointed at
isolated databases — embedded, ephemeral
containers, or per-job schemas.

8
00:01:09,143 --> 00:01:16,415
Shared staging schemas make parallel CI
jobs collide and produce ghosts: one job
deletes rows another just asserted.

9
00:01:16,535 --> 00:01:23,879
@DirtiesContext is a blunt hammer when
bean state leaks; prefer cleanup in
@BeforeEach and immutable test data ids.

10
00:01:23,999 --> 00:01:36,983
Transactional tests that roll back
automatically are convenient until you
assert on data visible only after commit —
know whether your test transaction wraps
the HTTP call. Runtime cost is the trade-
off you feel.

11
00:01:37,103 --> 00:01:49,415
Full context startup dominates wall time;
context caching across classes helps when
configurations match. Too many unique
@MockBean sets break that cache and CI
slows for invisible reasons.

12
00:01:49,535 --> 00:01:56,495
Symptom: “integration suite used to be
four minutes, now twenty” after every test
class invented its own mock set.

13
00:01:56,615 --> 00:02:09,287
Another runtime tell: a test passes with
MockMvc but fails on RANDOM_PORT because a
filter only runs on the real servlet path,
or because JSON dates serialize
differently through TestRestTemplate’s
message converters.

14
00:02:09,407 --> 00:02:18,383
Security and transactions are why this
layer exists. @WithMockUser proves
matchers; it does not prove JWT parsing.

15
00:02:18,503 --> 00:02:27,455
If gate’s booth scanners send bearer
tokens, add at least one test that builds
a signed test token — or accept that token
wiring lives in a narrower security test.

16
00:02:27,575 --> 00:02:37,943
For @Transactional on releaseGate, assert
both the happy ledger write and the empty
ledger after a mid-flow billing failure;
that is the integration bug @WebMvcTest
will never see.

17
00:02:38,063 --> 00:02:51,551
A misconception is replacing unit tests
with integration tests because “they catch
more.” They catch different things and
cost more CPU. Another is asserting only
status codes without ledger side effects.

18
00:02:51,671 --> 00:03:02,015
A third is @MockBean on so many types that
the test no longer integrates anything
meaningful. Mocked billing and in-memory
stores still skip the SQL dialect you
ship.

19
00:03:02,135 --> 00:03:08,400
When VesselRepository must prove derived
queries against real Postgres, reach for
Testcontainers.

20
00:03:08,519 --> 00:03:18,263
Keep a short matrix in the team’s head:
Mockito for tariff branches, @WebMvcTest
for JSON and validation, @SpringBootTest
for security-plus-ledger wiring,

21
00:03:18,383 --> 00:03:23,783
containers for SQL truth — each layer
answers a different lie the previous one
can tell.
